#doc (Manual) "Document and Edit Generator" =>

The {module}`VersoGen` library adds a document and edit sequence generator to Verso.
This can be used to test functional and non-functional aspects of Verso.

The generator starts with a datatype, {name VersoGen.Doc.SurfaceDoc.Part}`SurfaceDoc.Part`, that is the abstract syntax of Verso's concrete surface syntax.
That is, it contains things like roles and directives, and ensures that documents are well-nested.
This representation can be rendered to a string of Verso concrete syntax with {name VersoGen.Doc.concretizeDocument}`concretizeDocument`.
Next, a structural edit script ({name VersoGen.Edit.EditScript}`EditScript`, from {name VersoGen.Edit.generateScript}`generateScript`) is generated by traversing a zipper ({name VersoGen.Doc.Zipper}`Zipper`) over the document and occasionally performing a local mutation ({name VersoGen.Edit.Edit}`Edit`).
This results in a new document.
Both documents are rendered to strings, and a textual diff of the strings ({name VersoGen.TextEdit.textDiff}`textDiff`) is used to derive a set of LSP edits ({name VersoGen.TextEdit.toLspEdits}`toLspEdits`).
This LSP edit script can be replayed on the server ({name VersoGen.Server.reuseReport}`reuseReport`), with the resulting elaboration tasks checking the degree of re-use.
This will improve as we implement incrementality.

To support genre-specific elements in the generated documents, implement a genre profile ({name VersoGen.Genre.GenreProfile}`GenreProfile`).
The examples in `src/verso-gen/VersoGen/Genre`, {name VersoGen.Genre.minimalProfile}`minimalProfile` and {name VersoGen.Genre.manualProfile}`manualProfile`, demonstrate how to implement it.
